WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.199 --> 00:00:06.960
Landing a high-profile movie role doesn't
always guarantee you'll actually be featured

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:12.790
in the picture. Just ask these actors, who
were famously—or some might say infamously—fired

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:16.830
from their roles mere weeks, or even sometimes
days, after cameras started rolling. How could

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:20.570
that possibly happen? Well, prepare to see
some of your favorite movie characters in

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:22.380
a whole new light.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:23.820
Eric Stoltz

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:28.460
From the get-go, the team behind Back to the
Future wanted Michael J. Fox as Marty McFly.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:32.050
Only they couldn't have him, because he was
tied up shooting his hit show, Family Ties.

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:37.630
So, they went with another actor: Eric Stoltz.
According to Indiewire, director Robert Zemeckis

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:41.400
and producer Steven Spielberg realized weeks
into production that Stoltz wasn't getting

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:46.090
many laughs in rough cuts of the film. Plus,
he reportedly went full "method actor" on-set,

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.680
demanding that everyone refer to him as "Marty."

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:50.940
"Marty!"
"Marty, I'm sorry."

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:55.120
Co-star Tom Wilson stated that he got a particularly
aggravated experience during a fight scene

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:59.010
between Marty and his character, Biff Tannen.
Eventually, they fired Stoltz and brought

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:03.809
in their original choice. Re-casting Stoltz
ultimately proved to be a wise business decision.

00:01:03.809 --> 00:01:08.359
Back to the Future was a smash hit, and went
on to become a beloved classic. For his part,

00:01:08.359 --> 00:01:12.909
Stoltz told Moviehole in 2007 that getting
fired was a "freeing" experience in retrospect.

00:01:12.909 --> 00:01:15.170
Still, the situation sounds pretty heavy...

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:17.670
"There's that word again, heavy!"

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:19.209
Samantha Morton

00:01:19.209 --> 00:01:23.740
In the Oscar-winning movie Her, the computer
operating system that Joaquin Phoenix's character

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:28.799
falls in love with was initially voiced by
actress Samantha Morton. All went smoothly—until

00:01:28.799 --> 00:01:31.329
the film's team got to the editing room.

00:01:31.329 --> 00:01:35.719
Director Spike Jonze explained what happened
at the 2013 AFI Fest:

00:01:35.719 --> 00:01:40.060
"Samantha was really involved in giving Joaquin
[Phoenix] a lot…to work from. And then when

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:43.409
we got into editing, we realized that what
Samantha and I had done together wasn't working

00:01:43.409 --> 00:01:47.909
for what the character needed. So, we ended
up having to recast at that point in time."

00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:52.009
Morton's eventual replacement: Scarlett Johansson,
who filmed her dialogue around her Captain

00:01:52.009 --> 00:01:54.009
America: The Winter Soldier schedule.

00:01:54.009 --> 00:01:55.859
Harvey Keitel

00:01:55.859 --> 00:01:59.179
Apocalypse Now was plagued by production headaches.

00:01:59.179 --> 00:02:01.029
"The horror..."

00:02:01.029 --> 00:02:05.069
Among the worst: when director Francis Ford
Coppola had to fire his leading man, Harvey

00:02:05.069 --> 00:02:10.259
Keitel, shortly after the film started shooting.
Reports claim the two disagreed with the portrayal

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:15.250
of Keitel's character, Captain Benjamin Willard.
Keitel was replaced by Martin Sheen—who

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:18.930
suffered a heart attack during the infamously
difficult production—and Apocalypse Now

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:23.159
went on to massive commercial success and
nearly universal critical acclaim.

00:02:23.159 --> 00:02:24.459
Lori Petty

00:02:24.459 --> 00:02:29.760
Just two days into filming Demolition Man,
producer Joel Silver fired actress Lori Petty,

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.860
reportedly over "creative differences." Her
replacement: a young star named Sandra Bullock.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:38.659
Petty later spoke about her firing in a 2014
interview with The Daily Beast:

00:02:38.659 --> 00:02:43.129
"It was the most uncool day in Hollywood for
me. I just treat people the way I want to

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:46.349
be treated, so I’d rather not gossip about
his unkindness."

00:02:46.349 --> 00:02:47.810
Sean Young

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:52.390
Dick Tracy from 1990 originally had actress
Sean Young in the part of love interest, Tess

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:57.299
Trueheart, but she was replaced soon into
production. Why? A profile of Young by Entertainment

00:02:57.299 --> 00:03:00.110
Weekly said she was fired "for not seeming
maternal enough."

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:04.349
However, Young claimed the firing was a blessing,
telling the LA Times: "I don't think I could

00:03:04.349 --> 00:03:08.480
have survived working with Warren Beatty."
She later alleged in an interview with Movieline

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:12.239
she was fired for refusing Beatty's sexual
advances. For his part, Beatty has denied

00:03:12.239 --> 00:03:12.629
the accusations.

00:03:12.629 --> 00:03:15.409
What, she didn't like the jacket?

00:03:15.409 --> 00:03:18.469
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to watch more videos like the one

00:03:18.469 --> 00:03:21.810
you just saw. And leave us a comment to let
us know which fired performers should've made

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:22.870
the list...

